From b52f17b13d804321b5870c7ccdadca626af5a748 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 04:32:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] HDMI VRR left to the driver, or left off in
 every mode set Kodi makes

Kodi never flips with VRR: it never sets VRR_ENABLED, so the refresh of a mode stays fixed. NVKMS still
turns HDMI VRR on in every mode set of a display that declares it (from 50 Hz and 720 lines up, and from
110 % of the display's VRR minimum) and tells the display so with the EMP of HDMI VRR in every frame. That
gives Kodi nothing. And the black screen after a video, on the way back to 3840x2160 at 120 Hz over FRL on
an RTX 4080 SUPER, is not explained yet: HDMI VRR is on in that mode, and it is one of the things to rule
out.

A setting, "HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)" (System > Display, expert level), shown only where the
connector has NV_HDMI_VRR_OFF (nvidia-drm: every connector with a TMDS output from
nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9074 on). On, the default, leaves HDMI VRR to the driver, as before. Off asks
for NV_HDMI_VRR_OFF 1 in every mode set that Kodi makes, so the display is told that VRR is off; the
picture does not change. It applies with the next change of mode.

CHdmiVrrOff::Reason() says why a mode set asks for it: the setting first, then HDR10+ (Wanted()). With the
setting on, every decision is the one Kodi made before (a host test checks all 32 combinations of a mode
set, HDR10+, the kernel's value and native Dolby Vision); native Dolby Vision keeps owning the property
while it has the output. The setting is read only in a commit with a mode set. kodi.log says which
connector offers the choice and how it is set, and, for each change of the property, whether the setting
or HDR10+ left VRR off, or that the driver has it again. The comments that said the property exists only
on Turing and Ampere now say where it is.
